                           'by a primary key. 2NF requires 1NF compliance and '
                           'eliminates partial dependency, meaning every '
                           'non-prime attribute must depend on the whole '
                           'candidate key, not a proper subset of a composite '
                           'key. 3NF requires 2NF compliance and eliminates '
                           'transitive dependencies: non-key attributes must '
                           "depend solely on the candidate key ('X -> Y, where "
                           "Y is non-prime, requires X to be a superkey'). "
                           'BCNF (Boyce-Codd Normal Form) is a stricter '
                           'variant where every determinant X in functional '
                           'dependency X -> Y must be a superkey, resolving '
                           'anomalies with overlapping candidate keys. While '
                           '3NF/BCNF ensures data consistency and saves '
                           'storage, real-world analytical databases often '
                           'denormalize tables (e.g. Star Schema in OLAP) to '
                           'eliminate multi-table JOIN latency in read-heavy '
                           'queries.',
