                           'leads to underfitting, where the model performs '
                           'poorly on both training and test data. Variance '
                           'represents sensitivity to small fluctuations and '
                           'random noise in the training set; high variance '
                           'leads to overfitting, where the model achieves '
                           'near-zero training error but fails '
                           'catastrophically on unseen validation data. Total '
                           'Expected Error = Bias^2 + Variance + Irreducible '
                           'Error. To combat underfitting, practitioners '
                           'increase model capacity, add '
                           'polynomial/interaction features, or reduce '
                           'regularization. To prevent overfitting, '
                           'practitioners employ: 1) Regularization (L1 Lasso '
                           'for sparsity, L2 Ridge to penalize large weights), '
