                           'emulates virtual hardware (CPU, memory, NICs), '
                           'requiring every VM to boot an entire independent '
                           'guest operating system including its own kernel, '
                           'device drivers, and system services. This results '
                           'in gigabyte-scale disk images, minutes-long boot '
                           'times, and substantial RAM duplication. In '
                           'contrast, a container (Docker) virtualizes at the '
                           'operating system level. Containers running on a '
                           'host share the underlying host OS kernel. '
                           'Container isolation is achieved through native '
                           'Linux kernel primitives: 1) Namespaces provide '
                           'process visibility isolation (PID, Network, Mount, '
                           'UTS, IPC, User), ensuring a container cannot see '
                           'or interact with host processes. 2) Control Groups '
                           '(cgroups) meter and limit physical hardware '
                           'resource utilization (CPU shares, memory quotas, '
                           'I/O bandwidth). Containers package only '
                           'application code, binaries, and dependencies, '
                           'spinning up in milliseconds with megabyte-scale '
                           'footprints and near-native hardware execution '
