                           'layers to compute predictions and evaluate the '
                           'scalar loss function against ground-truth labels. '
                           '2) Backward Pass, where the calculus chain rule is '
                           'applied recursively in reverse order—from the '
                           'output layer back to the input layer—to compute '
                           'the partial derivative of the loss with respect to '
                           'each weight and bias tensor (nabla_W L). These '
                           'computed gradients are then used by optimization '
                           'algorithms (such as Adam or SGD) to update network '
                           'parameters. Historically, saturating activation '
                           'functions like Sigmoid and Tanh caused the '
                           'Vanishing Gradient Problem: the maximum derivative '
                           'of the standard Sigmoid function is only 0.25. '
                           'When chain-multiplying these fractional '
                           'derivatives across many layers, gradients diminish '
                           'exponentially toward zero, preventing early layers '
                           'from updating. The Rectified Linear Unit (ReLU: '
                           'f(x) = max(0, x)) resolves this because its '
                           'derivative is exactly 1.0 for all positive inputs, '
                           'allowing gradient signals to flow through deep '
                           'architectures without exponential attenuation.',
