                           'distinct design purposes: 1) Hierarchy & State: An '
                           "abstract class represents an 'is-a' relationship "
                           'and serves as a partially implemented base class; '
                           'it can declare non-static, non-final instance '
                           'fields, constructors, and private/protected '
                           'methods, maintaining internal object state. A '
                           'class can extend only one abstract class (single '
                           'inheritance). In contrast, an interface defines a '
                           "contract of capability or behavior ('can-do') that "
                           'unrelated classes can implement; all variables in '
                           'an interface are implicitly `public static final` '
                           'constants, and a class can implement multiple '
                           'interfaces. 2) Java 8+ Default Methods: Java 8 '
                           'introduced `default` and `static` methods to '
                           'interfaces, allowing method implementations within '
                           'interface bodies. This was engineered to provide '
                           'backward compatibility for the Collections '
                           'framework (enabling methods like `stream()` and '
                           '`forEach()` without breaking legacy implementing '
                           'classes). Choose an Abstract Class when sharing '
                           'mutable state, constructors, and internal helper '
                           'code across tightly related classes. Choose an '
                           'Interface when defining a polymorphic API contract '
                           'across diverse, unrelated classes.',
